  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThis approach leverages Chebyshev polynomial approximation and adaptive time-frequency analysis to represent the function h as a superposition of orthogonal modes. The core insight is that the optimal solution likely exhibits complex waveforms rather than simple piecewise constant structures. By decomposing the function into Chebyshev components, we efficiently represent complex structures that minimize overlap with its time-shifted counterpart. This is combined with adaptive sparse optimization along the energy-ranked modes to escape local minima.\n\nThe method works by:\n- Projecting the problem onto an orthogonal basis\n- Iteratively selecting modes that reduce the objective\n- Balancing model complexity and optimization time\n\nThis differs from direct grid optimization by focusing on structural properties rather than brute-force parameter tuning. It leverages mathematical insights from harmonic analysis to guide the search.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nRepresent h as coefficients in the Chebyshev series:\nh(x) = \u2211_{k=0}^{N-1} a_k T_k(x/2)\n\nWhere T_k are Chebyshev polynomials. The orthogonality ensures efficient representation. The objective becomes:\nC_5 = max_t { (1/2) \u2211_k \u2211_m a_k a_m T_k(t) T_m(t) } * dx/dx\n\nThe constraints become linear in the coefficients, so we directly optimize the coefficient vector with:\n- Element-wise box constraints on coefficients\n- Linear equality constraint on the sum\n\nBut note: Chebyshev coefficients can be outside [0,1]! We enforce the function constraint by:\nAfter optimization, apply inverse Chebyshev + projection\nh_values[i] = clip(T_h(2/ (N-1) * (i-count)), l,1)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nGrid focus:\nUse N=128 points. No need for powers-of-2 since Chebyshev already orthogonal.\n\nOptimization steps:\n1. Start simple: minimize using the provided objective but:\n- Use scipy.optimize.minimize with 'trust-constr' method\n- Tight constraints enforcement\n- Initial guess as provided parent with Chebyshev transform\n\n2. Then:\nExpand the basis: use sparse optimization - rank the Chebyshev indices by their energy contribution\n\n3. Optimization algorithm:\nUse sparse optimization using Boyd's multiplicative updates for the basis selection combined with constrained optimization\n\nConstraint handling:\nImplement element-wise projection after each iteration:\n- If any coefficient would yield h_value outside [0,1]:\nConvert to function, clip, and reproject coefficients\n\nProjection method:\nh_proj = np.clip(a_k_based + \u03bb * (constraint_gap), 0,1)\n\u03bb_tuned = arctanh(target/error) + base_thresholder\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\nStarting resolution: Always use N=128\nMode selection:\nStart with top-4 Chebyshev components (first 10 components per 10 trials)\n\nAdaptive sampling:\nAfter initial solution, iteratively:\n- Run 10 trials using random coefficient modifications\n- each trial: simulate adding/remove top-ranked modes\n\nTime allocation:\n70% time for constrained optimization\n30% time for adaptive mode selection\n\nFallback strategy:\nLowest-cost approach: use the provided parent\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness:\nDominant operations: \n- chebyshev transform: O(N*logN)\n- correlation computation: O(1) if precomputed with FFT\n- projection: O(N)\n\nTime estimation:\nN=128: about 0.3-0.5 seconds typically for optimization\n\nFailure handling:\nReduce mode selection tolerance by factor of 5\nUse dense algorithm if sparse fails to improve\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nMust check:\nh_values[i] in [0,1] for all i\nsum(h) * dx = 1 (within numeric tolerance)\n\nObjective value extracted by:\nc_full = np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode=\"full\")\nc_max = c_full.max()\nc5 = c_max * dx\n\nInclude helper to convert between coefficient arrays and h_values to ensure no floating-point blunders",
